<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dumat al-Jandal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Adummatu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dumat al-Jandal is an ancient oasis town in the Jawf region of northern Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, at the head of the Wadi Sirhan. Known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> as Adummatu, it was the centre of the Qedarite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and the shrine of their gods, whose images <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> carried off in the 7th century BCE. It was later a Nabataean and Roman outpost and fell to Muslim forces in the 7th century; the Mosque of Umar and Marid castle survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dumat-al-jandal.jpg" alt="Dumat al-Jandal" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nora Ali at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_old_city_of_Adummatu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
